  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ denote the height of an adult male, and let $Y$ denote the height of an adult female. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X<Y)=P(X-Y<0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(70,{4}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(65,{3.5}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-Y & ∼N(70-65,{4}^{2}+{3.5}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5,28.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X-Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{28.25}.$\n\nTo find $P(X-Y<0),$ we convert $X-Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-Y<0) & =P(Z<\\frac{0-5}{\\sqrt{28.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-0.94) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.94) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.94)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.94)≈0.1736.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-Y<0) & ≈Φ(-0.94) \\\\  & ≈0.1736.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that a woman is taller than a man is $0.1736.$",
